Shoals.Stochastic: the time horizon must be non-negative; sqrt of a negative horizon is NaN, so every path value would be NaN
Assumption: the guard refuses EVERY negative horizon, not merely a
conspicuously negative one. `sqrt` does not care about magnitude: at the
negative min subnormal -1.4e-45 the diffusion term is NaN exactly as it is at
t = -1, and the sampler returns NaN for every path.

Why this file exists separately from the other horizon fixtures, which all use
t = -1.0: it pins the guard's LOWER BOUNDARY, and nothing else in the suite
does. The positive-parity tests in tests/stochastic.ch pin t = 0 and t = -0.0
from above; the negative fixtures pin t = -1.0 from below; the interval between
was untested. A red-team round measured the consequence: the mutant
`gte(t, -0.01)` passes all 88 pre-existing negative fixtures AND both
positive-parity tests, while admitting t = -0.001 and returning NaN for all
eight paths -- i.e. it reintroduces shoals#139 in full with a green suite. Any
threshold in (-0.125, 0) survived that suite.

The horizon value is the SMALLEST-MAGNITUDE negative f32 for a measured reason,
not for emphasis. A first version of this fixture used -1e-6 and was itself
defeated: the mutant `gte(t, -1e-9)` REFUSES -1e-6, so the fixture passed and
that mutant survived at 89 ok / 0 failing. A fixture only kills thresholds
coarser than its own magnitude. Since `gte(-1.4e-45, x)` is true for every
negative f32 `x`, this value is admitted by ANY negative threshold and so
closes the whole family rather than a prefix of it.

That is the "do not trust green" case in this change: the guard's two
boundaries need pinning from BOTH sides, and a correct-looking family of
fixtures at one magnitude does not pin a threshold.

Deleting the guard does NOT make this file pass. The assertion is one that NaN
also fails, so it would then fail on the assertion rather than on the refusal,
`--expect neg` would see the wrong text, and the file would report
WRONG-DIAGNOSTIC.
